{-
   What a normalized call will not accept - `mapArguments`.

   # resolver: ownership

   A name nothing declares, a position filled twice, an argument written by position after one
   written by name, and a position left out that has nothing to fill it. The point of each is that it
   is one message naming the thing that is wrong, rather than the arity message a call whose count is
   perfectly fine would get.

   A construction goes through the same mapping and is here for that reason: `Pair {x: 1}` and
   `open(path: 1)` are one question asked of two declarations, so a rule that held for one and not
   the other would be the two copies this stage does not have.

   Reject.Default.yana is the declaration half - the positions a default may not be written on.
-}

data Pair {x: I32, y: I32 = 2}

fn open(path: I32, mode: I32 = 1, buffered: I32 = 2) -> I32 = path + mode + buffered


-- A name nothing declares. The count is right, so an arity message would be true and useless.
fn unknownName() -> I32 = open(1, format: 2)

-- The same parameter reached twice, once by position and once by name.
fn twice() -> I32 = open(1, 2, mode: 3)

-- An argument written by position after one written by name.
fn positionalAfterNamed() -> I32 = open(mode: 2, 5)

-- A position the count reached and the order did not: one argument is a number this signature
-- answers, and it filled the defaulted parameter because that is what a positional argument does.
fn middle(a: I32 = 1, b: I32) -> I32 = a * 10 + b
fn orderNotCount() -> I32 = middle(4)

-- The construction half of each: a field nothing declares, a field given twice, and one left out
-- that has no default.
fn badField() -> Pair = Pair {x: 1, z: 3}
fn fieldTwice() -> Pair = Pair {x: 1, x: 2}
fn missingField() -> Pair = Pair {y: 5}

fn main() -> I32 = open(1)
